  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front was located just north of our region this morning.\nA disturbance moving across the region was providing some\nshowers and thunderstorms. Showers and thunderstorms remain\npossible this morning, becoming more likely this afternoon and\nevening. The cold front will then push southward late today and\ntonight. \n\nMeso models showing plenty of 2 to 3 inch rainfall bullseyes,\nwith a couple over 4 inches. Models showing effective layer \nCAPE values of 1000 to 1500 developing over central West \nVirginia and extreme southern Ohio. With the ground being saturated \nat many locations, the flash flood threat remains high. Will \ncontinue the Flood Watch for flash flooding through tonight over\nnorthern and central portions of the region. \n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nModels showing effective layer CAPE values of 1000 to 1500 \ndeveloping over central West Virginia and extreme southern Ohio\nsouthward this afternoon. In addition, models also show some mid\nlevel dry air. Therefore damaging wind gusts, and possibly large\nhail, are possible will stronger thunderstorms this afternoon\nand evening. Even outside of severe thunderstorms, the wet\nground could allow sub-severe winds to uproot trees. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA cold front will push south of the area Wednesday night. This \nwill bring drier air behind the front Thursday, continuing for \nFriday, with a break from the precipitation. \n\nA moisture starved reinforcing front will push through on \nSaturday. Once again most of the area will remain dry, with the\ndry weather continuing on Sunday and most likely into the next \nwork week. \n\nWith the drier air behind these fronts, nighttime temperatures\nwill be considerably cooler, with readings generally in the 50s,\nwith some cooler locations dipping into the 40s.",
